## <div style="background-color:#1a5276; color:white; padding:15px; text-align:center; font-family: 'Arial Black', Gadget, sans-serif;">
Lesson Planner – Theme # What Is Renewable Energy? – Lesson # 2
</div>
<div style="background-color:#f0f0f0; color:#333; padding:8px; text-align:center; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 0.9em;">
Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Renewable Energy in Panama</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Is Renewable Energy?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Interpret written instructions for daily activities. Reading: Recognize the language on a simple form asking for personal information.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret instructions on saving energy in daily routines (e.g., "To save energy, you should turn off the TV when you're not watching, and unplug chargers when they're not in use.")<br>Can identify and fill in the required information in surveys and forms related to renewable energy in Panama (e.g., "Indicate if your home uses solar panels," "yes or no," or "Check whether your home uses LED bulbs or traditional bulbs.")<br>Can interpret steps in a guide on setting up home energy-saving habits (e.g., "Summertime instructions on how to unplug devices at school and reduce fan or A/C use during the day.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color:#1a5276; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; color:#1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; color:#1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; color:#1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color:#1a5276; margin-top:0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a few pictures related to energy consumption (e.g., a light bulb on, a TV on with no one watching, a phone charger plugged in) and asks students to identify actions. "What is happening in this picture? Is it saving energy or wasting it?" (e.g., "Turning off lights is saving energy."). Students use vocabulary from Lesson 1 (e.g., <i>energy, consume, save, unplug</i>).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple instruction on the board, like "Unplug your phone charger when it's full." The teacher models reading it clearly, pointing out key words like 'unplug' and 'when'. The teacher also models the stress in words like "unplug" and "energy" while reviewing pronunciation from the previous lesson.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If my phone is at 100%, should I leave the charger plugged in?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'unplug' mean?" (Expected answer: "To take out the plug from the socket.")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color:#1a5276; margin-top:0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students will read a short informational text titled "Panama's Energy-Saving Habits Guide" which includes a set of instructions for daily routines and a simple survey form regarding home energy use. The text will feature a clear genre (instructions, survey), register (informative, formal but accessible), and format (bullet points for instructions, checkboxes for survey). It includes grammar (gerunds: "Saving energy is...", present continuous: "We are using...", adverbial clauses: "because it preserves...") and vocabulary (<i>clean, renewable, solar, wind, efficiency, consume, unplug, preserve, sustainability</i>).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student interprets various types of written texts, including instructions and simple forms, to extract key information and understand daily activities related to energy saving.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the "Panama's Energy-Saving Habits Guide" silently.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the main idea of this guide?" (Expected: "It's about saving energy in Panama.") "Why is saving energy important, according to the text?" (Expected: "Because it preserves natural resources and helps the environment.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students decide if statements about the instructions are true or false (e.g., "The guide suggests turning off lights when you leave a room. True/False").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match an energy-saving action from the instructions with its benefit mentioned in the text (e.g., "Unplugging devices" with "Reduces phantom power consumption").</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color:#1a5276; margin-top:0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension focused on identifying instructions and specific information in forms, practicing target vocabulary (<i>generate, provide, justify, harness, efficiency, sustainability, conservation, solar, wind, resource, clean, renewable</i>) and grammar (gerunds, present continuous, adverbial clauses for justification like 'because it preserves').</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Activity):</b> The teacher provides a text with missing key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>energy, solar, clean, efficiency, unplug, conserve</i>) and verb forms, directly from the "Panama's Energy-Saving Habits Guide." Students complete the gaps, focusing on identifying the correct word based on context. Example: "___ energy is important for the future." (Saving). "We are currently ___ more solar panels." (installing).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Instruction Scramble):</b> Students receive sentences related to energy saving instructions but with the words in scrambled order. They must put them in the correct order to form coherent instructions (e.g., "off / turn / TV / the / you're / not / watching / when"). This reinforces reading and understanding of instructional structure. Another activity: Match the first part of an instruction with its logical ending. E.g., "Renewable energy is vital..." with "...because it preserves natural resources."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Form Completion & Cross-Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a slightly different version of the simple energy survey form from the presentation stage (e.g., one has questions, the other has possible answers). They read their form and verbally exchange information to complete a combined form, using sentences like "My home uses LED bulbs, yes or no?" or "I think solar panels are good because they generate clean energy." They then check each other's completed forms for accuracy, comparing their interpretations of the instructions and questions.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color:#1a5276; margin-top:0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will apply their reading skills to interpret and respond to a 'Community Energy Survey'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group work / Individual Response. Students, in groups of three, are given a more detailed "Community Energy Survey for Panama Homes." This survey includes sections with instructions on how to fill it out (e.g., "Check all that apply," "Write your answer in the space provided") and questions asking for personal information related to renewable energy use and energy-saving habits (e.g., "Does your home use any renewable energy sources? If yes, which ones?," "How often do you unplug devices?," "Indicate if your home uses solar panels: Yes/No"). Students must read the instructions carefully, interpret each question, and individually fill out the survey based on their own homes or a hypothetical scenario provided by the teacher. They discuss within their groups to clarify any instructions or vocabulary, practicing explaining and justifying their answers using phrases like, "We are using LED bulbs because they are more efficient," or "I unplug devices because it saves electricity." The focus is on interpreting written questions and instructions to provide accurate information.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color:#1a5276; margin-top:0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and review of completed individual surveys.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students hand in their individually completed "Community Energy Survey." The teacher quickly reviews forms for comprehension and accuracy.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret written instructions (e.g., followed "check all that apply").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying and filling in required information.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>solar, panels, efficiency, save, electricity</i>).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in written responses.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one instruction about saving energy that you learned today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you now in reading instructions on a form (1=not confident, 5=very confident)?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color:#1a5276; margin-top:0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of interpreting the instructions or filling out the survey?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new vocabulary word or phrase about renewable energy that you used today?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The reading activities allowed most students to interpret basic instructions and forms. The connection to "Renewable Energy in Panama" was clear and engaging for students.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with more complex adverbial clauses for justification within the text. I need to provide more explicit scaffolding for identifying cause-and-effect relationships in instructions.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), I will plan activities that encourage students to verbally justify their energy choices and discuss the benefits of renewable energy, building on the vocabulary and concepts from today's reading.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, students will actively speak and interact using the target vocabulary and grammatical structures to discuss energy-saving habits and the importance of renewable energy, preparing for a mini-debate or group discussion.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>